        C("Erythrovirus pathophysiology and presentation", `<p>Parvovirus B19 is an <strong>Erythroparvovirus</strong> that infects human erythroid precursor cells. Incubation is <strong>4–14 days</strong>; transmission occurs through respiratory secretions, saliva, placenta, and blood products.</p><p>Children develop fifth disease: fiery <span class="hl">slapped cheeks</span>, circumoral pallor, then a lacy evanescent rash on trunk and limbs after fever resolves.</p>`, true),
        C("Erythrovirus diagnosis, treatment, and prevention", `<table class="learn-table"><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Lecture point</th></tr><tr><td>Diagnosis</td><td>Usually clinical; IgM or serum/bone-marrow PCR; RT-PCR preferred if immunocompromised</td></tr><tr><td>Treatment</td><td>Supportive; transfusion may be considered for aplastic crisis; IVIG may be considered in immunocompromised patients</td></tr><tr><td>Prevention</td><td>Handwashing; infected patients avoid pregnant people</td></tr></table><p>Major risks are aplastic crisis/pure red-cell aplasia in immunosuppression or hemolytic disease and fetal hydrops/loss in pregnancy.</p>`),
        C("Measles pathophysiology and recognition", `<p>Measles (rubeola) is a single-stranded RNA Morbillivirus spread by droplets. It is highly contagious, incubates about <strong>10 days</strong>, and is most common in winter/spring.</p><p>High fever, malaise, coryza, dry cough, and conjunctivitis precede <strong>Koplik spots</strong>—table-salt lesions opposite the molars that are pathognomonic.</p><p>Two days later, a brick-red blanching maculopapular rash begins behind the ears/hairline and moves <span class="hl">downward and outward</span>.</p>`, true),
        C("Measles diagnosis, treatment, and prevention", `<table class="learn-table"><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Lecture point</th></tr><tr><td>Diagnosis</td><td>Clinical plus confirmation with IgM/ELISA, fourfold antibody rise, viral culture, or fluorescent antibody staining</td></tr><tr><td>Treatment</td><td>Supportive; isolate for 1 week after rash onset; vitamin A 200,000 units/day × 2 days in selected pediatric disease</td></tr><tr><td>Prevention</td><td>MMR; avoid live vaccine in pregnancy and immunocompromise, except asymptomatic HIV on ART</td></tr></table><p>Complications include otitis media, pneumonia, encephalitis, and subacute sclerosing panencephalitis 5–15 years later.</p>`),
        C("Mumps pathophysiology and recognition", `<p>Mumps is an extremely contagious Paramyxovirus spread mainly by respiratory droplets and saliva. Incubation is <strong>10–21 days</strong>; outbreaks favor schools and colleges in late winter/spring.</p><p>Within 48 hours of headache, myalgias, fatigue, anorexia, and malaise, <span class="hl">tender parotitis</span> develops, often asymmetric first but then bilateral. Stensen ducts may be red and prominent; trismus can occur.</p>`, true),
        C("Mumps diagnosis, treatment, and prevention", `<table class="learn-table"><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Lecture point</th></tr><tr><td>Diagnosis</td><td>Clinical; salivary-duct swab with NAAT; culture if unavailable; mild leukopenia/lymphocytosis and elevated amylase</td></tr><tr><td>Complication clues</td><td>High fever → consider meningitis or orchitis/oophoritis; epigastric pain → pancreatitis</td></tr><tr><td>Treatment</td><td>Supportive; isolate until swelling subsides, about 9 days; bedrest until afebrile</td></tr><tr><td>Prevention</td><td>MMR; same pregnancy/immunocompromise limits as measles</td></tr></table>`),
        C("Rubella pathophysiology and recognition", `<p>Rubella (German measles/third disease) is a Rubivirus spread by inhaled droplets with a <strong>16-day incubation</strong>. It is transmissible from 1 week before rash until 15 days after resolution.</p><p>Postnatal disease is asymptomatic in about half. Tender suboccipital, posterior cervical, and postauricular nodes may precede a fine pink maculopapular rash that spreads rapidly over 2–3 days and often fades in 1 day. Polyarticular arthritis is most common in adults.</p>`, true),
        C("Rubella diagnosis, treatment, prevention, and pregnancy", `<table class="learn-table"><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Lecture point</th></tr><tr><td>Acute diagnosis</td><td>Elevated IgM, fourfold IgG rise, or virus isolation; isolated IgM can be false positive</td></tr><tr><td>Past immunity</td><td>Positive IgG indicates prior infection or vaccination</td></tr><tr><td>Treatment</td><td>Supportive</td></tr><tr><td>Prevention</td><td>MMR; postpartum vaccination for susceptible patients</td></tr></table><p>First-trimester fetal infection leads to congenital rubella in 80% of cases. Cataracts, glaucoma, microphthalmia, deafness, cardiac defects, developmental impairment, organomegaly, and blueberry-muffin rash can occur.</p>`),
        C("Coxsackievirus pathophysiology and recognition", `<p>Coxsackievirus is an Enterovirus in Picornaviridae. It affects children under 5–7, spreads mainly fecal-orally, incubates <strong>3–5 days</strong>, and peaks in summer/early autumn.</p><table class="learn-table"><tr><th>Syndrome</th><th>Distinctive presentation</th></tr><tr><td>Hand-foot-mouth</td><td>Low fever; tongue/buccal lesions; nonpruritic rash on hands, feet, buttocks, limbs</td></tr><tr><td>Herpangina</td><td>High fever 102–104°F; sore throat; posterior papulovesicles that ulcerate</td></tr></table>`, true),
        C("Coxsackievirus diagnosis, treatment, and prevention", `<table class="learn-table"><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Lecture point</th></tr><tr><td>Diagnosis</td><td>Clinical; PCR preferred from throat, stool, or vesicle fluid in severe disease</td></tr><tr><td>Treatment</td><td>Supportive, antipyretics and fluids; acyclovir does not work because the virus lacks thymidine kinase</td></tr><tr><td>Course</td><td>HFMD resolves in 7–10 days; herpangina fever 2–4 days and lesions 5–6 days</td></tr><tr><td>Prevention</td><td>Handwashing, contact precautions/isolation if hospitalized; no U.S. vaccine</td></tr></table>`),
        C("SARS-CoV-2 pathophysiology and presentation", `<p>SARS-CoV-2 is a single-stranded RNA <strong>betacoronavirus</strong> that binds ACE2, especially in lung epithelium and upper airway.</p><p>Primary spread is respiratory/airborne. Incubation is usually <strong>4–5 days</strong>, up to 14; infectiousness is greatest a few days before symptoms and for about 10 days after symptoms begin.</p><p>Nasal congestion, cough, sore throat, myalgias, headache, diarrhea, dysgeusia, and anosmia occur; pneumonia adds fever, productive cough, and dyspnea.</p>`, true),
        C("SARS-CoV-2 diagnosis and treatment", `<table class="learn-table"><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Lecture point</th></tr><tr><td>Diagnosis</td><td>NAAT/RT-PCR or antigen; lymphopenia; elevated inflammatory markers; ground-glass opacities/consolidation</td></tr><tr><td>Outpatient high-risk</td><td>Paxlovid if symptoms began under 5 days; consider age 75+, immunocompromise, multiple comorbidities</td></tr><tr><td>Paxlovid cautions</td><td>Severe hepatic impairment, drug interactions, renal dosing</td></tr><tr><td>Hospital oxygen/ventilation</td><td>Dexamethasone and/or remdesivir; VTE prophylaxis</td></tr></table>`),
        C("SARS-CoV-2 prevention and outcomes", `<p>Prevention includes vaccination, ventilation, handwashing, respiratory hygiene, isolation during peak illness, and monoclonal antibodies for severely immunocompromised patients.</p><p>Complications include bacterial, fungal, and viral secondary infections and <strong>Long COVID</strong>: symptoms lasting beyond 3 months, including fatigue, dyspnea, chest pain, cough, mood symptoms, memory/concentration problems, and insomnia.</p>`)
